2,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,50,70206,4930,22,65,61,18,29776,17,1124,50853,320,19,65,61,17,29776,19999,18,2137,3,2661,429,400,65,3,374,2477,36929,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSimplify $(7b^3)^2 \\cdot (4b^2)^{-3},$ given that $b$ is non-zero.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f74571d727fa58e9f516e2fd08edbd56daade47ee18808326ee8ec615e61e35f"},"seconds":14.16709017753601,"seed":632323577835679550,"stop":"eos","text":"To simplify the expression \\((7b^3)^2 \\cdot (4b^2)^{-3}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Simplify each part of the expression separately.**\n2. **Combine the simplified parts.**\n3. **Use the properties of exponents to further simplify the expression.**\n\nLet's start with the first part: \\((7b^3)^2\\).\n\nUsing the property of exponents \\((xy)^n = x^n y^n\\), we get:\n\\[\n(7b^3)^2 = 7^2 (b^3)^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(7^2\\):\n\\[\n7^2 = 49\n\\]\n\nThen, we simplify \\((b^3)^2\\):\n\\[\n(b^3)^2 = b^{3 \\cdot 2} = b^6\n\\]\n\nSo, \\((7b^3)^2 = 49b^6\\).\n\nNow, let's simplify the second part: \\((4b^2)^{-3}\\).\n\nUsing the property of exponents \\((xy)^n = x^n y^n\\), we get:\n\\[\n(4b^2)^{-3} = 4^{-3} (b^2)^{-3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(4^{-3}\\):\n\\[\n4^{-3} = \\frac{1}{4^3} = \\frac{1}{64}\n\\]\n\nThen, we simplify \\((b^2)^{-3}\\):\n\\[\n(b^2)^{-3} = b^{2 \\cdot (-3)} = b^{-6}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\((4b^2)^{-3} = \\frac{1}{64} b^{-6}\\).\n\nNow we combine the two simplified parts:\n\\[\n(7b^3)^2 \\cdot (4b^2)^{-3} = 49b^6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{64} b^{-6}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the constants and the exponents:\n\\[\n49 \\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\cdot b^6 \\cdot b^{-6} = \\frac{49}{64} \\cdot b^{6 + (-6)} = \\frac{49}{64} \\cdot b^0\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b^0 = 1\\) for any non-zero \\(b\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{49}{64} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{49}{64}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{49}{64}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{49}{64}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:1143"}
